response:
 A new subcommittee of 3 members of the Senate committee will be randomly chosen. To find the probability of a subcommittee made of 3 Republicans we need the total number of ways a subcommittee of 3 can be chosen and how many choices of 3 Republicans there are. Notice that there are 5 Republicans (denoted as R1, R2,...R5) in the Senate committee and we need to choose three of them. The number of choices is (5,3) , that is to say, $\left(\begin{array}{c}
5\\
3
\end{array}\right)=\frac{5!}{3!\, 2!}=10$. To find the total number of ways we could choose a subcommittee of 3 members of the Senate committee we notice that there are 13 members of the committee and we have to choose three. The total number of ways is (13,3). That is to say, $\left(\begin{array}{c}
13\\
3
\end{array}\right)=\frac{13!}{3!\, 10!}=286$ Now the the probability of getting a subcommittee consisting of only Republicans is $\left(\begin{array}{c}
5\\
3
\end{array}\right)\div \left(\begin{array}{c}
13\\
3
\end{array}\right)=\frac{5!}{3!\, 2!}\;\frac{10!\, 3!}{13!}=\frac{10}{286}=\frac{5}{143}$. <answer> $\frac{5}{143}$ </answer>
